(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-axial-musculature-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"The Axial Musculature — Anatomy Exam 4 — Set 3","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"The muscles of facial expression are innervated by which cranial nerve?","choices":["The trigeminal nerve","The hypoglossal nerve","The facial nerve","The accessory nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The muscles of facial expression are supplied by the facial nerve (cranial nerve seven). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which sphincter-like muscle encircles the eye and closes the eyelids?","choices":["The orbicularis oris","The orbicularis oculi","The buccinator muscle","The zygomaticus major"],"answer":1,"exp":"The orbicularis oculi encircles the eye and closes the eyelids in blinking and squinting. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which muscle encircles the mouth and purses or closes the lips, the 'kissing muscle'?","choices":["The orbicularis oculi","The orbicularis oris","The buccinator muscle","The occipitofrontalis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The orbicularis oris encircles the mouth and closes and purses the lips. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which deep cheek muscle compresses the cheek against the teeth, aiding in chewing and blowing?","choices":["The masseter muscle","The buccinator","The temporalis muscle","The platysma muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The buccinator forms the muscular wall of the cheek and compresses it against the teeth, keeping food between the molars and aiding blowing. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"The four muscles of mastication are innervated by a branch of which cranial nerve?","choices":["The facial nerve","The vagus nerve","The abducens nerve","The trigeminal nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"The muscles of mastication are supplied by the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve five). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which powerful muscle over the angle of the jaw is the prime elevator of the mandible in chewing?","choices":["The buccinator muscle","The platysma muscle","The mylohyoid muscle","The masseter muscle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The masseter overlies the ramus and angle of the mandible and is a powerful elevator of the mandible during chewing. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which fan-shaped muscle of the temporal fossa elevates and retracts the mandible?","choices":["The masseter muscle","The temporalis","The medial pterygoid","The lateral pterygoid"],"answer":1,"exp":"The temporalis is the fan-shaped muscle of the temporal fossa that elevates and retracts the mandible. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How many extra-ocular muscles move each eyeball?","choices":["Four muscles","Eight muscles","Two muscles","Six muscles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Six extra-ocular muscles move each eyeball: four rectus muscles and two oblique muscles. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which extra-ocular muscle turns the eye laterally (abducts it) and is supplied by the abducens nerve?","choices":["The medial rectus","The superior rectus","The lateral rectus","The inferior oblique"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lateral rectus abducts the eye and is supplied by the abducens nerve (cranial nerve six). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"The muscles of the tongue are mostly innervated by which cranial nerve?","choices":["The facial nerve","The trigeminal nerve","The hypoglossal nerve","The abducens nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Most tongue muscles are supplied by the hypoglossal nerve (cranial nerve twelve); the palatoglossus is the exception, supplied by the vagus. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"How are the tongue muscles that change its shape, rather than its position, classified?","choices":["As extrinsic tongue muscles","As muscles of mastication","As pharyngeal constrictors","As intrinsic tongue muscles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Intrinsic tongue muscles lie entirely within the tongue and change its shape, while extrinsic muscles move its position. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which large paired neck muscle flexes the neck and turns the head to the opposite side?","choices":["The sternocleidomastoid","The masseter muscle","The buccinator muscle","The diaphragm muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sternocleidomastoid flexes the neck and rotates the head to the opposite side; acting together the pair flex the neck. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"The infrahyoid muscles, which lie below the hyoid bone, are also known by what common name?","choices":["The strap muscles","The mastication muscles","The rotator cuff muscles","The pelvic floor muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"The infrahyoid muscles are called the strap muscles because of their thin, ribbon-like shape below the hyoid bone. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"The three pharyngeal constrictor muscles function mainly to do what during swallowing?","choices":["Elevate the eyebrows in surprise","Squeeze food downward into the esophagus","Rotate the eyeball outward","Compress the cheek against teeth"],"answer":1,"exp":"The superior, middle, and inferior pharyngeal constrictors contract in sequence to squeeze the food bolus down toward the esophagus during swallowing. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"The axial muscles are those that act on which parts of the body?","choices":["The head, neck, and trunk","The arms and hands only","The legs and feet only","The pectoral girdle only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Axial muscles act on the axial skeleton — the head, neck, and trunk — rather than on the limbs. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which muscle raises the eyebrows and wrinkles the forehead, covering the top of the skull?","choices":["The orbicularis oris","The buccinator muscle","The masseter muscle","The occipitofrontalis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The occipitofrontalis (epicranius) covers the scalp and raises the eyebrows, wrinkling the forehead in surprise. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which muscle of mastication protrudes the mandible and helps open the mouth?","choices":["The temporalis muscle","The masseter muscle","The lateral pterygoid","The medial pterygoid"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lateral pterygoid protrudes the mandible and depresses it to help open the mouth. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"The suprahyoid muscles lie in which position relative to the hyoid bone?","choices":["Below the hyoid bone","Inside the thoracic cavity","Above the hyoid bone","Within the orbit of the eye"],"answer":2,"exp":"The suprahyoid muscles lie above the hyoid bone and elevate it or depress the mandible during swallowing and speech. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which extra-ocular muscle turns the eye medially (adducts it) toward the nose?","choices":["The lateral rectus","The medial rectus","The superior oblique","The inferior rectus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The medial rectus adducts the eye, turning the pupil toward the nose. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which extrinsic tongue muscle protrudes (sticks out) the tongue?","choices":["The styloglossus muscle","The hyoglossus muscle","The genioglossus","The masseter muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The genioglossus, arising from the mandible, protrudes the tongue and depresses its center. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"The pharyngeal constrictor muscles are innervated mainly by which cranial nerve?","choices":["The hypoglossal nerve","The facial nerve","The trochlear nerve","The vagus nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pharyngeal constrictors are supplied mainly by the vagus nerve (cranial nerve ten) through the pharyngeal plexus. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which broad, thin muscle sheet of the neck tenses the skin and depresses the lower lip?","choices":["The masseter muscle","The platysma muscle","The temporalis muscle","The buccinator muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The platysma is a broad, thin sheet in the superficial neck that tenses the skin and pulls down the lower lip and corner of the mouth. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which paired muscle of mastication, deep to the ramus, elevates the mandible along with the masseter?","choices":["The medial pterygoid","The lateral pterygoid","The occipitofrontalis","The orbicularis oculi"],"answer":0,"exp":"The medial pterygoid lies deep to the mandibular ramus and elevates the mandible, working with the masseter. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"The sternocleidomastoid receives its motor innervation from which cranial nerve?","choices":["The accessory nerve","The facial nerve","The hypoglossal nerve","The trigeminal nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sternocleidomastoid is supplied by the accessory nerve (cranial nerve eleven), which also supplies the trapezius. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which extra-ocular muscle is innervated by the trochlear nerve and passes through a pulley of connective tissue?","choices":["The lateral rectus","The superior oblique","The medial rectus","The inferior rectus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The superior oblique passes through the trochlea, a fibrous pulley, and is supplied by the trochlear nerve (cranial nerve four). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which tongue muscle is the exception, supplied by the vagus nerve rather than the hypoglossal nerve?","choices":["The palatoglossus","The genioglossus muscle","The hyoglossus muscle","The styloglossus muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The palatoglossus is the exception among tongue muscles, supplied by the vagus nerve rather than the hypoglossal nerve. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which muscle draws the angle of the mouth upward and laterally, as in smiling?","choices":["The orbicularis oculi","The buccinator muscle","The platysma muscle","The zygomaticus major"],"answer":3,"exp":"The zygomaticus major runs from the zygomatic bone to the corner of the mouth and draws it up and out in smiling. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Muscles of the axial group include those of facial expression, mastication, the neck, and which body wall region?","choices":["The forearm and hand","The gluteal region","The trunk and abdomen","The leg and foot"],"answer":2,"exp":"Axial muscles include those of facial expression, mastication, the neck, and the trunk, including the abdominal and back walls. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How many paired muscles of mastication move the mandible?","choices":["Four paired muscles","Two paired muscles","Six paired muscles","Eight paired muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"There are four paired muscles of mastication: the masseter, temporalis, medial pterygoid, and lateral pterygoid. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve supplies most of the extra-ocular muscles, except the lateral rectus and superior oblique?","choices":["The oculomotor nerve","The abducens nerve","The trochlear nerve","The trigeminal nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve three) supplies four of the six extra-ocular muscles, all except the lateral rectus and superior oblique. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"}]};
